    "status": "owner-supplied reference; individual numerical claims not independently verified",
    "content": "# Culinary Substitution & \"Cooking Sense\" Rules\nReference data for Rinet's substitute-ingredient suggestion engine (Margin Guard), compiled from culinary substitution guides, chef-sourced advice, and food-science sources (2025–2026).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core Substitution Principle\n\nBefore suggesting any substitute, classify the ingredient's **role in the dish**:\n\n| Role | Examples | Substitution risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| Flavor/aroma accent (finishing touch, small quantity) | Truffle, saffron, fresh herbs, citrus zest | **Low risk** — easiest to substitute, since the dish's structure doesn't depend on it |\n| Structural (binds, leavens, emulsifies) | Eggs in a soufflé, gluten in bread dough, sugar in caramel | **High risk** — substitution changes the dish's physical behavior, not just its flavor |\n| Bulk/body ingredient | Rice, pasta, potatoes, ground meat | **Medium risk** — substitutable, but changes texture and cook time |\n\n**Rule for Rinet's engine:** only auto-suggest substitutes for low-risk (flavor/accent) ingredients with confidence. Medium-risk substitutions should be surfaced as an option with a clear \"this will change texture/cook time\" flag. High-risk structural ingredients (eggs in a soufflé, yeast in bread) should generally NOT be auto-suggested for substitution — flag the price spike, but let the owner decide, since a bad structural swap can ruin the dish entirely.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Luxury / High-Cost Ingredient Substitutes\n\nThe exact scenario Rinet needs to handle well: an expensive specialty ingredient spikes in price, and the system should suggest a cheaper alternative with an honest note on the flavor trade-off.\n\n| Expensive ingredient | Approx. cost | Substitute(s) | Savings | Flavor trade-off note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fresh black/white truffle | ~$800–1,400/lb | Truffle oil (finishing only) | ~90%+ | Most truffle oil is synthetic aroma, not real truffle — use sparingly as a finish, not in cooking |\n| Fresh black/white truffle | ~$800–1,400/lb | Dried porcini, rehydrated + truffle salt | ~85% | Earthier, less refined, but genuinely good in risotto, pasta, mac & cheese |\n| Fresh truffle | — | Black trumpet mushroom (\"poor man's truffle\") | ~90%+ | Different flavor profile — use lightly to avoid overpowering |\n| Saffron | ~$1,000+/lb | Turmeric (dried or fresh) | ~99% | Near-identical color; flavor is milder/earthier, not truly equivalent — best where saffron is mainly used for color |\n| Pine nuts | ~$1.50/oz | Equal-parts cashew + almond + remaining pine nuts | ~50% | Retains buttery texture, dilutes the distinct pine flavor |\n| Wagyu / premium steak cuts | High | Shoulder tender, flat iron, or other lesser-known cuts | Significant | Sear + finish in oven; less marbling means less margin for overcooking |\n| Fresh goat cheese (chèvre) | Moderate-high | Cream cheese, loosened with plain yogurt | ~50–60% | Loses the tang somewhat — a splash of lemon juice or vinegar helps restore it |\n| Imported/aged balsamic | High | Standard balsamic reduced with a touch of sugar or honey | ~80%+ | Less complex, but works fine in vinaigrettes and glazes |\n| Fresh porcini | ~$5–8/oz | Baby bella (cremini) mushrooms | ~90%+ | Milder flavor — boost with a splash of soy sauce or a pinch of MSG/umami seasoning |\n\n**General principle from professional sources:** potent, high-flavor-impact ingredients (truffle, saffron, aged cheeses, cured meats) can often be used in **smaller quantities than a recipe calls for** without a noticeable flavor loss — before suggesting a full substitute, consider suggesting a reduced quantity of the original as a first, lower-risk option.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Everyday Substitution Ratios (for common ingredient-availability alerts, not just luxury items)\n\n| Original | Substitute | Ratio |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fresh herbs | Dried herbs | 3:1 (1 tbsp fresh = 1 tsp dried) |\n| Butter (baking) | Vegetable oil | 3:4 (1 cup butter = 3/4 cup oil) — makes baked goods moister |\n| Butter (baking) | Applesauce | 1:1 — reduces fat ~75%, makes results denser |\n| Buttermilk | Milk + lemon juice/vinegar | 1 cup milk + 1 tbsp acid, rest 5 min |\n| Whole egg (baking, non-structural) | Aquafaba (chickpea liquid) | 3 tbsp per egg |\n| Whole egg (baking) | Unsweetened applesauce | 1/4 cup per egg — moisture/binding only, no leavening power |\n| Fresh garlic (1 clove) | Garlic powder | 1/8 tsp |\n| Fresh ginger (1 tbsp) | Ground ginger | 1/4 tsp — much more concentrated |\n| Heavy cream (1 cup) | Milk + melted butter | 7/8 cup milk + 2 tbsp butter |\n| Cornstarch (1 tbsp, thickening) | All-purpose flour | 2–3 tbsp |\n| Chicken broth | Vegetable broth, or water + soy sauce | 1:1 |\n| Cake flour (1 cup) | All-purpose flour minus 2 tbsp, plus 2 tbsp cornstarch | — |\n\n**Note on herbs specifically:** delicate herbs (cilantro, tarragon, chives, basil) lose much of their character when dried and substitute poorly — the 3:1 ratio works better for robust herbs (rosemary, thyme, oregano).\n\n---\n\n## 4. When NOT to Substitute\n\nFlag these dish types as **substitution-sensitive** — the system should warn rather than auto-suggest:\n- Meringues, soufflés (egg structure is load-bearing)\n- Custards, crème brûlée (precise egg/dairy/sugar ratios)\n- Yeast breads (gluten development is central to the result)\n- Caramel / sugar work (sugar chemistry doesn't tolerate substitution well)\n- Whipped cream from a lower-fat substitute (won't whip properly)\n- Laminated pastry (precise fat/flour ratio and temperature-dependent structure)\n\n---\n\n## 5. Cost-vs-Flavor Scoring Logic (suggested framework for the substitute engine)\n\nWhen ranking substitute candidates for a price-spiked ingredient, score each option on:\n1. **Cost savings %** — how much cheaper is the substitute per equivalent portion\n2. **Flavor proximity** — low/medium/high similarity to the original (use the notes above as a starting rubric)\n3. **Role risk** — flavor-accent (safe), bulk (moderate), structural (flag, don't auto-suggest)\n4. **Availability** — is it something the restaurant likely already stocks or can easily source, vs. a specialty order\n\nA good suggestion typically has high cost savings + high-or-medium flavor proximity + low role risk. A substitute that saves money but scores poorly on flavor proximity for a signature dish should be presented as an option with a clear caveat, not a confident recommendation — the owner, not the AI, should make the final call on anything that could change a dish customers already know and expect.\n"
